  "id": "mediterranean",
  "name": "Mediterranean",
  "tagline": "Hot and open, but it makes you stop — a long straight into slow corners",
  "difficulty": "Fast",
  "notes": [
    "Retraced. The first version had five corners and none tighter than 159 m -- it measured as an oval and drove like one, flat out the whole way with nothing to get wrong.",
    "Still the fast circuit: an 800 m straight and sweepers out to 600 m radius. But there are now corners under 130 m to brake for, because a lap with no slow corner has no rhythm -- nothing on it is a decision.",
    "Traced from straights and arcs with the closure solved by least squares over EVERY straight, so the radii are the ones that were designed and the correction is a few metres spread across a dozen straights rather than hundreds loaded onto two. Picking two to absorb it does not work here at all: with this many big sweepers the arcs carry the loop most of the way round on their own, and every pair asks for a negative straight.",
    "Elevation is invented: two gentle waves over the lap."
  ],
